      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-n/q10-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Rearrange tan 4x −tan 2x = 0 as tan 4x = tan 2x. For defined tangents, their equality means 4x −2x = 180◦n, [B1] [A1] so x = 90◦n. [M1] [A1] Both tangents are defined at all four candidates in the given interval: 0◦, 90◦, 180◦, 270◦. [M1] [M1] [A1] Alternative working. Use tan(2θ) = 2 tan θ/(1 −tan2 θ) with θ = 2x. Put u = tan 2x. Where the original tangents exist, the double-angle formula gives 2u 2u −u(1 −u2) = 0 u(1 + u2) = 0. 1 −u2 −u = 0 =⇒ =⇒ Since 1 + u2 &gt; 0 for real u, tan 2x = 0, so 2x = 180◦n, giving the same four angles:\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
