    "id": "madas/alevel-pure-n/q11",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-n/q11-question-1.svg\" alt=\"The st rd th 1 , 3 and 11 term of an arithmetic progression are the first three terms of a geometric progression. It is further given that the sum of the first 13 terms of the arithmetic progression is 260 . Find, in any order, the common ratio of the geometric progression and the first term and common difference of the arithmetic progression. ( ) 8\" style=\"--madas-width:596.0px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-n/q11-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Starting the modelling with a diagram: Let a, d, r be the first term, common difference and common ratio. [M1] The terms u1, u3, u11 are a, a + 2d, a + 10d. Hence ar = a + 2d and (a + 2d)r = a + 10d. Eliminating r, the geometric relationship gives (a + 2d)2 = a(a + 10d), and expanding yields a2 + 4ad + 4d2 = a2 + 10ad\" style=\"--madas-width:628.0px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-n/q11-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"4d2 = 6ad 2d2 = 3ad which simplifies to 2d(2d −3a) = 0. [A1] The arithmetic sum is S13 = 13 2 [2a + (13 −1)d]. Also 13(a + 6d) = 260, [A1] so a + 6d = 20. If d = 0, [A1] then a = 20, r = 1 . [A1] If 2d = 3a, [A1] then 6d = 9a. Substitute in a + 6d = 20: a = 20 −9a 10a = 20 a = 2 d = 3 ar = a + 2d 2r = 2 + 6\" style=\"--madas-width:678.7px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-n/q11-solution-3.svg\" alt=\"r = 4 Thus a = 2, d = 3, r = 4 . Both triples satisfy the original conditions: [A1] The handwritten solution lists the nonconstant progression: [B1]\" style=\"--madas-width:680.0px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 11 is 8 marks.</p>"
    ],
